  "id": "Compound::DB06292 - Gene::6524",
  "verbalization": "Dapagliflozin, a sodium-glucose transporter 2 (Sglt2) inhibitor, is linked to Slc5a2 through multiple pathways. The primary mechanism involves its interaction with Slc5a2, as evidenced by the direct binding of Dapagliflozin to Slc5a2, which is a key component of the sodium-glucose cotransporter pathway. This relationship is further supported by the inclusion of Dapagliflozin in the class of Sglt2 inhibitors, which are known to target Slc5a2. Additionally, the shared lowest common ancestor between Dapagliflozin and Canagliflozin, both Sglt2 inhibitors, reinforces the broader context of their interaction with the transporter family.\n\nComplementary evidence from Path 2 shows that Dapagliflozin also binds to Slc5a4, a member of the Na+-dependent glucose transporter family, which is closely related to Slc5a2. This suggests a broader role for Dapagliflozin in modulating glucose transporters, potentially influencing Slc5a2 through shared functional pathways. While the direct binding to Slc5a2 is established, the indirect involvement through Slc5a4 highlights the complexity of the transporter network.\n\nThe integration of these findings suggests that Dapagliflozin exerts its effects on Slc5a2 through direct interaction, possibly by inhibiting the transporter's function, which in turn affects glucose reabsorption in the kidneys. This mechanism is consistent across multiple pathways, reinforcing the role of Dapagliflozin in modulating glucose transporters and their downstream effects.",
